# Writing Project: [Your Title Here]

This file is read by the Claude writing commands (`.claude/commands/`) to understand
your project's structure. Place it at the root of your writing project repository.

---

## Drafts

Where your draft files live and how they are named.

- **Directory:** `Chapter drafts/`
- **Naming pattern:** `Chapter {N} - Draft.md`
- **Critique suffix:** ` - Critique` (appended before `.md`, e.g. `Chapter 1 - Draft - Critique.md`)
- **Read-through suffix:** ` - Read-Through` (appended before `.md`, e.g. `Chapter 1 - Draft - Read-Through.md`)
- **Continuity log:** `Continuity Check.md`

---

## Reference Documents

List the files that are the canon / source of truth for this project. When critiquing
drafts, these documents take precedence over the prose. Commands will read all of them
when checking continuity, character consistency, and world rules.

- `Characters.md` — character profiles, psychology, voice, relationships
- `The World.md` — worldbuilding, geography, society, and physical environment
- `Magic System.md` — rules for any magic, technology, or special abilities

> Add or remove entries as needed. Short descriptions help the AI understand what
> each file covers.

---

## Story Structure

Paths to your planning documents. Leave blank or remove a line if you don't have
that type of document.

- **Chapter plan:** `Chapter Plan.md` (one entry per chapter: POV, purpose, themes)
- **Beat plans:** `Chapter Writing Plans.md` (beat-by-beat plans per chapter or section)

---

## Epigraph Traditions (optional)

Define the source traditions or styles for chapter epigraphs. If this section is
absent, the `/epigraph` command will ask you to describe the voice before generating
candidates.

For each tradition, describe its voice, register, and attribution format. Three
traditions are shown below as examples — replace, remove, or add as fits your project.

### The Historical Record
Voice and register: Dry, institutional, past-tense. Written as if from an archivist
or chronicler. Slightly ironic distance from events described.
Attribution format: [Name], [Institution or Title], [Date or Period]
Thematic territory: Consequence, record, the gap between official history and lived truth.

### The Folk Voice
Voice and register: Oral, collective. Uses "we" or "they" as if passing down
received wisdom. Compressed, rhythmic, weighted with what's left unsaid.
Attribution format: None, or simply "Traditional" / "Origin unknown"
Thematic territory: Community memory, loss, the things people carry.

### The Personal Letter
Voice and register: Addressed to someone who may not exist. Intimate, slightly
desperate, occasionally dark. Written in second person.
Attribution format: Unsigned, or signed with a first name only.
Thematic territory: Grief, longing, things said too late.
